-- Listening Lesson 10 step by step (IELTS-10 v0.3.0), steps 4-6 of 6: five guided steps and one mixed check, in the Lesson 6 format
-- (LEARN FIRST worked example, an explanation for every answer, the transcript only after submitting). Audio is cut
-- from the owner-approved lesson recording (no new speech) and stored in the private ielts-targeted-listening bucket
-- by exact SHA-256. Owner instruction 2026-10-28 15:17 (Asia/Bishkek): 'Go next'. Named-student pilot with
-- requires_review; nothing is released. Source: docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_LISTENING_06.json.
do $steps$
declare tF constant text := 'LECTURER: Good morning, everyone. In today''s lecture I want to look at something we all experience but rarely think about: waiting in queues. Businesses spend a surprising amount of effort designing how we wait, and many of the principles behind these designs go back to a well-known paper on the psychology of waiting lines, published in the nineteen-eighties.
LECTURER: Let''s start with the shape of the queue itself. Many banks and post offices once had a separate line for each counter. The problem was that customers kept comparing their own line with the others, and they became frustrated whenever another line moved faster. A single line feeding several counters solves this. The average wait may be almost the same, but people consider the system fairer, and that sense of fairness makes the wait easier to accept.
LECTURER: This leads to an important point. What matters to customers is often not the actual length of the wait, but how long it feels. Last term I tried an informal poll with one of my seminar groups. I asked them to guess how long they had queued in the canteen that day, and then we compared their guesses with the times on their receipts. I''d predicted that the guesses would be about fifty per cent too long, but in fact most people guessed roughly double their real wait. That was one small group, so treat it as an illustration rather than a research finding, but it does show how unreliable our sense of waiting time can be. So the question for designers is what makes a wait feel longer than it really is, and several factors stand out.
LECTURER: The first is having nothing to do. Unoccupied time drags. A well-known example comes from office buildings where people complained that the lifts were too slow. Rather than install faster lifts, which would have been expensive, managers put mirrors beside the lift doors. People spent the time checking their appearance, and the complaints largely stopped, even though the lifts were no faster.
LECTURER: The second factor is uncertainty. If you don''t know how long you''ll be waiting, every minute seems longer. That''s why many theme parks and call centres now display or announce an estimated waiting time. Interestingly, some organisations deliberately make that estimate a little longer than they expect the wait to be, so that customers are pleasantly surprised when they''re served early.
LECTURER: Third, unexplained waits feel longer than explained ones. Passengers on a delayed train are far more patient when the driver gives the reason for the delay, even if the news itself is bad. Silence, on the other hand, tends to make people imagine the worst.
LECTURER: The fourth factor concerns the stage of the process. Waiting before a service begins feels longer than waiting once it has started. Restaurants understand this very well. Rather than leaving customers standing by the door until a table is ready, they often hand them a menu, because once you''re choosing your meal, you feel the experience has already begun.
LECTURER: A further factor, and one that is easy to overlook, is company. People who wait alone consistently report the time as longer than people who wait with friends or family. Conversation is a natural distraction, and it can work even between strangers, which is why some waiting areas now arrange their seating so that people can talk more easily.
LECTURER: The last factor, and perhaps the most powerful, is fairness. People will tolerate a long wait if everyone is treated equally, but even a short wait becomes unbearable if someone pushes in. Seeing someone jump the queue produces anger out of all proportion to the time actually lost.
LECTURER: Finally, let''s think about the future of queuing. Increasingly, the line itself is disappearing. In virtual queues, customers register when they arrive and are then told when it''s their turn. Early systems handed out electronic pagers, but so many of these went missing that most venues now simply send a message to the customer''s own phone. This allows people to shop, eat or simply sit down instead of standing in line. It doesn''t remove the wait, of course, but it changes how the wait feels, which, as we''ve seen, is what really matters.
LECTURER: In the next lecture, we''ll look at how these ideas have been applied in hospitals, where waiting can be a matter of health rather than convenience.';
begin
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,audio_path,audio_sha256,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-listening-l18-v1','0.1.0','Listening Lesson 10 step 4 of 6: The lecturer''s main argument','listening','guided_practice','targeted-listening-06/a5331a1cfeeb7128eaf2f600bda7931adff4215cec9e47098dd9eef11ce6d5dd/l18/BH-TL06-L10F_v1.mp3','a5331a1cfeeb7128eaf2f600bda7931adff4215cec9e47098dd9eef11ce6d5dd','You will hear part of a lecture about the design of queues. Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then listen to the whole lecture and choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D. You may pause or replay.','Main-idea questions ask what the lecturer is arguing, not a single detail. Listen for evaluation words: ''perhaps the most powerful'', ''what really matters'', ''easy to overlook''.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "What is the lecturer''s main argument?", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "472c05b5-8cc0-43b8-b300-ae5b769d7769", "supporting_node_id": "4bdeaffb-7390-4b00-9955-69b9da537007", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  How long a wait feels matters more than how long it really is."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  Customers prefer separate lines for each counter."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Businesses should always make queues shorter."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  Virtual queues have removed waiting altogether."}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Which factor does the lecturer think is perhaps the most powerful?", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "4bdeaffb-7390-4b00-9955-69b9da537007", "supporting_node_id": "472c05b5-8cc0-43b8-b300-ae5b769d7769", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  having nothing to do"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  company"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  fairness"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  uncertainty"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Which factor does the lecturer say is easy to overlook?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "4bdeaffb-7390-4b00-9955-69b9da537007", "supporting_node_id": "59fbf038-1ceb-48b0-b905-b7f78a8076e3", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  fairness"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  company"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  the stage of the process"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  uncertainty"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "What does the lecturer find interesting about estimated waiting times?", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "4bdeaffb-7390-4b00-9955-69b9da537007", "supporting_node_id": "19e4299c-70f4-4d30-8726-4416be272056", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Some organisations make them longer on purpose."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  They are only used in theme parks."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  Customers rarely notice them."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  They are usually wrong."}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "According to the lecturer, what happens when there is silence about a delay?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "8818c0d5-823d-491f-a73f-b2adda0c4127", "supporting_node_id": "610d8325-5a22-4df9-beb0-aa512ff82374", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  People leave the queue."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  People tend to imagine the worst."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  People ask for a reason."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  People become more patient."}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "What does the lecturer say about seeing someone jump the queue?", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "4bdeaffb-7390-4b00-9955-69b9da537007", "supporting_node_id": "44773835-b0ba-4e99-a9a5-c11a0d02bdaf", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  Most people do not notice it."}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  It rarely happens in well-designed queues."}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  It causes anger far greater than the time actually lost."}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  It only matters when the wait is long."}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS listening exercise; not a full paper or an official IELTS recording.", "suggested_minutes": 9, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-listening-targeted-focus-v1", "passage": "LEARN FIRST: listen for the argument, not just the facts\n\nA lecture has facts and examples, but it also has an ARGUMENT: the main point the lecturer wants you to remember.\nListen for words that evaluate:\n- importance: the most powerful, an important point, what really matters\n- surprise: interestingly, surprisingly\n- what people miss: easy to overlook\n- a conclusion that links back: as we''ve seen ...\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nYou hear: ''A further factor, and one that is easy to overlook, is company.''\n''Easy to overlook'' tells you the lecturer thinks people often forget this factor.\n\nNow listen to the whole lecture and answer six questions.", "focus": "Identify main spoken points and opinions: follow the lecturer''s argument and evaluation across a whole talk."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('teacher_notes', '1 A, The lecture returns to this at the end: "It doesn''t remove the wait, of course, but it changes how the wait feels, which, as we''ve seen, is what really matters."
2 C, "The last factor, and perhaps the most powerful, is fairness."
3 B, "A further factor, and one that is easy to overlook, is company."
4 A, "Interestingly, some organisations deliberately make that estimate a little longer than they expect the wait to be, so that customers are pleasantly surprised".
5 B, "Silence, on the other hand, tends to make people imagine the worst."
6 C, "Seeing someone jump the queue produces anger out of all proportion to the time actually lost."
Read each explanation, then listen to that part again and hear the proof yourself.

Transcript of what you heard:
' || tF),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text and recording; no third-party passages, questions or audio copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_LISTENING_06.json 0.1.0", "audio_file": {"file": "BH-TL06-L10F_v1.mp3", "sha256": "a5331a1cfeeb7128eaf2f600bda7931adff4215cec9e47098dd9eef11ce6d5dd", "bytes": 6671971, "duration_seconds": 277.968, "contains": "copyright notice, 10 s silence, the whole lecture, without the question cues, 2 s silence", "derived_from": {"file": "IELTS-10-L1_v2.mp3", "sha256": "02b8031fc265cce24760adb645830d198308254a81695c56039e98a876132c9e", "passage_seconds": [49.528, 311.145], "owner_approval": "Owner listening approval of the source recording, 2026-10-27 18:09 Asia/Bishkek"}, "method": "Cut from the approved recording with ffmpeg; no new speech; MP3 192 kbps, 48 kHz mono, copyright metadata", "human_review": "pending: owner listens to this exact file"}}'::jsonb,'51e9174319025c82ad370fc2252578fae4587a45f08d76d73ddc6346ccd4f409',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,audio_path,audio_sha256,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-listening-l19-v1','0.1.0','Listening Lesson 10 step 5 of 6: Match the example to the factor','listening','guided_practice','targeted-listening-06/a5331a1cfeeb7128eaf2f600bda7931adff4215cec9e47098dd9eef11ce6d5dd/l19/BH-TL06-L10F_v1.mp3','a5331a1cfeeb7128eaf2f600bda7931adff4215cec9e47098dd9eef11ce6d5dd','You will hear part of a lecture about the design of queues. Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then listen to the whole lecture. Questions 1-6 are examples from the lecture. For each one, choose the factor it illustrates: the correct letter, A-F. You may pause or replay.','Each example comes straight after the factor it illustrates. Note the factor when you hear its signpost, then match the example that follows.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "mirrors beside lift doors", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "5e1150ab-ce8c-4037-88cd-0c89cb6632fb", "supporting_node_id": "610d8325-5a22-4df9-beb0-aa512ff82374", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  unfairness"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  before the service starts"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  having nothing to do"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  waiting alone"}, {"value": "E", "label": "E  not knowing how long"}, {"value": "F", "label": "F  no explanation"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "estimated waiting times at theme parks", "accepted_answers": ["E"], "taxonomy_node_id": "5e1150ab-ce8c-4037-88cd-0c89cb6632fb", "supporting_node_id": "610d8325-5a22-4df9-beb0-aa512ff82374", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  unfairness"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  before the service starts"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  having nothing to do"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  waiting alone"}, {"value": "E", "label": "E  not knowing how long"}, {"value": "F", "label": "F  no explanation"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "a driver telling passengers why a train is delayed", "accepted_answers": ["F"], "taxonomy_node_id": "5e1150ab-ce8c-4037-88cd-0c89cb6632fb", "supporting_node_id": "610d8325-5a22-4df9-beb0-aa512ff82374", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  unfairness"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  before the service starts"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  having nothing to do"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  waiting alone"}, {"value": "E", "label": "E  not knowing how long"}, {"value": "F", "label": "F  no explanation"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "menus given to people waiting for a table", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "5e1150ab-ce8c-4037-88cd-0c89cb6632fb", "supporting_node_id": "610d8325-5a22-4df9-beb0-aa512ff82374", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  unfairness"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  before the service starts"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  having nothing to do"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  waiting alone"}, {"value": "E", "label": "E  not knowing how long"}, {"value": "F", "label": "F  no explanation"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "seats arranged so that people can talk", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "5e1150ab-ce8c-4037-88cd-0c89cb6632fb", "supporting_node_id": "610d8325-5a22-4df9-beb0-aa512ff82374", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  unfairness"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  before the service starts"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  having nothing to do"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  waiting alone"}, {"value": "E", "label": "E  not knowing how long"}, {"value": "F", "label": "F  no explanation"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "someone pushing in", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "5e1150ab-ce8c-4037-88cd-0c89cb6632fb", "supporting_node_id": "610d8325-5a22-4df9-beb0-aa512ff82374", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  unfairness"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  before the service starts"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  having nothing to do"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  waiting alone"}, {"value": "E", "label": "E  not knowing how long"}, {"value": "F", "label": "F  no explanation"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS listening exercise; not a full paper or an official IELTS recording.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-listening-targeted-focus-v1", "passage": "LEARN FIRST: an example belongs to the point before it\n\nLecturers give a point, then an example to illustrate it. To match them:\n1. Write the factors down as you hear their signposts (the first is ..., the second factor is ...).\n2. When you hear ''for example'', ''that''s why'' or ''understand this very well'', the example belongs to the factor you just heard.\n3. Do not match by single words: the example may use different words from the factor.\n\nThe six factors in this lecture:\nA  unfairness\nB  before the service starts\nC  having nothing to do\nD  waiting alone\nE  not knowing how long\nF  no explanation\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nYou hear: ''Restaurants understand this very well.'' This comes straight after the point about waiting before a service begins.\nSo the restaurant example illustrates B.\n\nNow listen and match six examples.", "focus": "Track spoken reference and logical relationships: link each example to the point it illustrates."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('teacher_notes', '1 C: the mirrors come after "The first is having nothing to do. Unoccupied time drags."
2 E: they come after "The second factor is uncertainty." Not knowing how long = uncertainty.
3 F: it follows "Third, unexplained waits feel longer than explained ones."
4 B: it follows "Waiting before a service begins feels longer than waiting once it has started. Restaurants understand this very well."
5 D: it belongs to company: "People who wait alone consistently report the time as longer".
6 A: "even a short wait becomes unbearable if someone pushes in". This is the fairness factor.
Read each explanation, then listen to that part again and hear the proof yourself.

